				<form id="hero-search-form" action="/search" method="get" role="search">
					<label for="hero-search-input" class="visually-hidden">Search for your city, county, utility, or state</label>
					<div class="hero-search-row">
						<input type="search" id="hero-search-input" name="q" placeholder="Enter your city, county, utility, or state…" autocomplete="off" />
						<button type="submit" class="hero-search-btn">Search →</button>
					</div>
				</form>
				<p class="hero-search-examples">Try: <a href="/search?q=Austin">Austin, TX</a> · <a href="/search?q=Sacramento">Sacramento, CA</a> · <a href="/search?q=Ohio">Ohio</a> · <a href="/permits">or browse all 50 states →</a></p>

				<div class="hero-stats">
					<div class="stat-item"><span class="stat-number">50</span><span class="stat-label">States With Research on File</span></div>
					<div class="stat-divider"></div>
					<div class="stat-item"><span class="stat-number">33</span><span class="stat-label">States With Published Guides</span></div>
					<div class="stat-divider"></div>
					<div class="stat-item"><span class="stat-number">404</span><span class="stat-label">Verified Locality Records</span></div>
				</div>
				<p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #94a3b8; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
					Coverage depth varies by state — California is by far our deepest, with 288 published city guides. Where we don't yet have enough verified information to publish a full guide, we say so rather than guessing. See our <a href="/methodology" style="color:#93c5fd;">Methodology</a> for exactly how a page earns "published" status.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="hero-trust-card">
				<h2>What makes a GridPermit guide different</h2>
